  "media_type": "image",
  "explanation": "<strong>Explanation: </strong>This is an unusual rainbow. For one thing, the rainbow is not complete, appearing only in a sunlit <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Brise_Fan_LACMA_M.78.108.10.jpg\">fan</a> -- but that by itself is not very unusual. What is most unusual is the fake location of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">the Sun</a>. The Sun appears to be toward the front, just below those hills -- but it is not. That is not <a href=\"https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/about/k-12-education/optical-phenomena/what-causes-rainbow\">how rainbows work</a>. Rainbows involve a backward <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(physics)\">reflection</a> from falling <a href=\"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/atmosphere/rain\">rain</a>, so that the Sun must really be <a href=\"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/1*t6nZgti5Q6Bp5Gm7zPK7vA.jpeg\">behind you</a>. In this case, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-july-27-crepuscular-moon-rays-over-denmark/\">gaps between clouds</a> near the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-june-15-two-worlds-one-sun/\">setting Sun</a> allowed rays to cross the entire sky and appear to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-june-24-anticrepuscular-rays-converge-opposite-the-sun/\">converge in front</a>, an unusual phenomenon called <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-25-anticrepuscular-rays-over-sicily/\">anticrepuscular rays</a>. The photo was taken with a phone last week at sunset in <a href=\"https://youtu.be/_KIIMuEaM-0?t=24\">Presenzano</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>. The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-february-4-anticrepuscular-rays-a-rainbow-fan-over-spain/\">seemingly reverse rainbow</a> was noticed when <a href=\"https://www.rmets.org/metmatters/ten-ways-dry-laundry-outside-all-year-round\">hanging up laundry</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>space ghosts",
  "copyright": "<a href=\"mailto:meryormella at gmail dot com\">Aurelia Degli Esposti</a>",
  "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg?w=5120&h=3840&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint",
  "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 20 - An Anticrepuscular Rainbow Fan</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-20-an-anticrepuscular-rainbow-fan/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: This is an unusual rainbow. For one thing, the rainbow is not complete, appearing only in a sunlit fan -- but that by itself is not very unusual. What is most unusual is the fake location of the Sun. The Sun appears to be toward the front, just below those hills -- but it is not. That is not how rainbows work. Rainbows involve a backward reflection from falling rain, so that the Sun must really be behind you. In this case, gaps between clouds near the setting Sun allowed rays to cross the entire sky and appear to converge in front, an unusual phenomenon called anticrepuscular rays. The photo was taken with a phone last week at sunset in Presenzano, Italy. The seemingly reverse rainbow was noticed when hanging up laundry.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: space ghosts\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"an anticrepuscular rainbow fan\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"An Anticrepuscular Rainbow Fan\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-20-an-anticrepuscular-rainbow-fan/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg?w=5120&#038;h=3840&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg?w=5120&#038;h=3840&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Hills are seen in the distance below a partly cloudy sky. A group of orange rays appears to eminate from one area behind the hills. Across this fan of rays, near the top of the image, a short rainbow can be seen.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-20T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-21T21:31:43-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"An Anticrepuscular Rainbow Fan\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg?w=5120&#038;h=3840&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 20<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg\" alt=\"Hills are seen in the distance below a partly cloudy sky. A group of orange rays appears to eminate from one area behind the hills. Across this fan of rays, near the top of the image, a short rainbow can be seen.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> An Anticrepuscular Rainbow Fan </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"mailto:meryormella at gmail dot com\">Aurelia Degli Esposti</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation: </strong>This is an unusual rainbow. For one thing, the rainbow is not complete, appearing only in a sunlit <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Brise_Fan_LACMA_M.78.108.10.jpg\">fan</a> -- but that by itself is not very unusual. What is most unusual is the fake location of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">the Sun</a>. The Sun appears to be toward the front, just below those hills -- but it is not. That is not <a href=\"https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/about/k-12-education/optical-phenomena/what-causes-rainbow\">how rainbows work</a>. Rainbows involve a backward <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(physics)\">reflection</a> from falling <a href=\"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/atmosphere/rain\">rain</a>, so that the Sun must really be <a href=\"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/1*t6nZgti5Q6Bp5Gm7zPK7vA.jpeg\">behind you</a>. In this case, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-july-27-crepuscular-moon-rays-over-denmark/\">gaps between clouds</a> near the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-june-15-two-worlds-one-sun/\">setting Sun</a> allowed rays to cross the entire sky and appear to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-june-24-anticrepuscular-rays-converge-opposite-the-sun/\">converge in front</a>, an unusual phenomenon called <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-25-anticrepuscular-rays-over-sicily/\">anticrepuscular rays</a>. The photo was taken with a phone last week at sunset in <a href=\"https://youtu.be/_KIIMuEaM-0?t=24\">Presenzano</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>. The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-february-4-anticrepuscular-rays-a-rainbow-fan-over-spain/\">seemingly reverse rainbow</a> was noticed when <a href=\"https://www.rmets.org/metmatters/ten-ways-dry-laundry-outside-all-year-round\">hanging up laundry</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>space ghosts\n<p> <center>\n\n<b> Tomorrow's picture: </b><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-21-new-ghosts-revealed-in-cassiopeia/\">APOD: 2026 October 21 &#8211; New Ghosts Revealed in Cassiopeia</a>\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-19-treble-clef-unusual-x-ray-hot-radio-source/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-21-new-ghosts-revealed-in-cassiopeia/\">&gt;</a>\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>"
